<h2>The prosecution case</h2>
<p>The allegation was grave. On 6 November 2013, the accused — the victim's maternal uncle — was said to have taken the minor girl from the house. A missing report was lodged on 12 November 2013, and the accused was later traced at Siliguri. An FIR of 13 May 2014 recorded his alleged disclosure that he had raped and strangled the child. Bones and teeth were recovered on 15 May 2014, in a recovery attributed to his disclosure under Section 27 of the Indian Evidence Act, 1872.</p>
<p>The Sessions Court convicted under Sections 376(2)(i), 302 and 201 of the IPC and Sections 4 and 6 of the POCSO Act, 2012, imposing imprisonment for life. The High Court of Sikkim affirmed. Before the Supreme Court, the entire edifice was circumstantial: no eyewitness, no body identified in the ordinary way — a chain of inferences from disappearance to disclosure to recovery.</p>

<h2>Where the chain snapped</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Last seen — assumed, not seen.</strong> No witness deposed to seeing the accused take the victim. The foundational circumstance was inference layered on inference.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>The recovery — from open ground.</strong> The spot of the Section 27 recovery was visible and accessible to the public, demanding a caution the courts below had not applied.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>The DNA report — fatally inconsistent.</strong> The report recorded the bone extracts as being of male origin. The victim was female. The one piece of scientific evidence contradicted the prosecution's own narrative.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>The FSL report — inconclusive.</strong> The forensic analysis did not connect the recovered articles to the victim or to the accused with any certainty.</div>
</div>
<p>The Court held that the guilt of the appellant had not been established beyond reasonable doubt, that the chain of circumstances was broken at various places, and that the accused's statement under Section 313 CrPC could not be pressed into service to repair it. The convictions were set aside and the appellant ordered to be released forthwith unless required in another case.</p>

<h2>The larger lesson for POCSO and murder trials</h2>
<p>Nothing in the judgment dilutes the seriousness with which courts treat sexual offences against children. What it polices is method. The POCSO Act contains statutory presumptions under Sections 29 and 30, but presumptions operate only after foundational facts are proved; they do not relieve the prosecution of establishing, by admissible evidence, that the offence occurred and that the accused committed it. Where the case is circumstantial, the five golden principles governing such evidence apply with full rigour, and scientific evidence that contradicts the charge cannot simply be passed over because the accusation is dreadful.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The DNA finding deserves particular attention from practitioners. Defence counsel frequently treat forensic reports as the prosecution's territory. This case is a reminder that the defence must read every scientific exhibit closely: the decisive contradiction — male bone extracts in the case of a female victim — was sitting in the record through two rounds of conviction.</p>
</div>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>In circumstantial prosecutions, test each link independently: last seen, motive, disclosure, recovery, scientific corroboration. A single broken link is not a technicality; it is the difference between proof and suspicion.</p>
<p>Scrutinise Section 27 recoveries for exclusivity of knowledge — recoveries from publicly accessible places carry reduced evidentiary weight.</p>
<p>Cross-check forensic reports against the prosecution narrative line by line; inconsistencies of identity, origin or chronology are acquittal-grade material.</p>
<p>Remember the appellate route: even concurrent findings of two courts yield where the record discloses perversity in the appreciation of evidence — the Supreme Court's jurisdiction under Article 136 extends to exactly such cases.</p>
</div>
